      "writtenOn": "2024-01-02T20:10:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m surprised this works (or does it?) since the 8-stop gradient shader assumed trailing unused stops are equal to the last stop:\nhttps://crsrc.org/c/third_party/skia/src/sksl/sksl_graphite_frag.sksl;l\u003d491;drc\u003d4f218069",
      "revId": "6d5d7e8b6a5eeb9c9cefc1342833aff48b7274e6",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6b6cccde_869102f9",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/KeyHelpers.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 327,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-02T21:09:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ah good catch. I think it still works because by the time this function is called, `t.x` is clamped to be in `[0, 1]`. I believe the last valid offset in the array will have a value equal to `1` because we aren\u0027t using implicit starting and ending offsets.\n\nThe way the code is written isn\u0027t ideal, but when `t.x \u003d 1` we should be taking the `else` branch that mixes the last valid stop and the now-zero\u0027ed padding. The mix parameter will be `0 / -1` so should still be selecting the last valid stop (assuming that `mix(valid stop, half4(0), 0)`) does not somehow introduce error in the valid stop color value.\n\nWe could update shaders to use `\u003c\u003d` in the ifs so that we always mix up to the last valid stop, or I can go back to copying the last value. `memset` was easier here because of of the type change from a scalar array to two Vec4s. WDYT?",
      "parentUuid": "a438e6f3_4320266a",
      "revId": "6d5d7e8b6a5eeb9c9cefc1342833aff48b7274e6",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "809f97f1_b1b0168a",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/KeyHelpers.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 327,
      "author": {
        "id": 13824
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-02T22:06:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Using `\u003c\u003d` in the shaders we\u0027d need to be careful about cases where we end with a hard stop so the last two valid stops are at t\u003d1. I\u0027d have to think about it more but I know it\u0027s not as simple as changing all the `\u003c`s to `\u003c\u003d`.\n\nOn a related note, we probably want to filter out redundant stops in gradients (if we aren\u0027t already), following up on\nhttps://skia-review.googlesource.com/c/skia/+/541725/comment/59ff5020_a94979ba/",
      "parentUuid": "6b6cccde_869102f9",
      "revId": "6d5d7e8b6a5eeb9c9cefc1342833aff48b7274e6",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "eaa7c64b_0bdeffe9",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/KeyHelpers.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 327,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-03T16:54:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027ve switched back to preserving the last color and offset in the arrays to avoid these complications. It ended up being pretty simple with the `rawOffsets` pointer.\n\nI\u0027m not sure if we do any redundant stop removal at a higher level. Ganesh has(d?) some code to merge offsets that were too close to each other because of how it encoded data as scaleAndBias terms in half-precision. \n\nThere definitely can be stops that have the same offset as each other for a hard stop. Unless you\u0027re referring specifically to the case where there are 3 stops at the same offset and the middle color will never be visible then?",
      "parentUuid": "809f97f1_b1b0168a",
      "revId": "6d5d7e8b6a5eeb9c9cefc1342833aff48b7274e6",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
